<slide>
  text-size: 2.4vmin
</slide>

<h2>Slides, sections, blocks and clicks</h2>

<p>
  A <b>slide</b> is one <c>.slide</c> file made of tags: text (<c><h2></c>, <c><p></c>, <c><ul></c>...), images, charts and <b>code blocks</b>, in reading order.
  A <c><code></c> is one animated block, and its <c><frame></c>s are the states it passes through, one click each:
</p>

<code lang=text>
  <code lang=ts id=fn>
    <frame>let a = 1;</frame>         <- block "fn", state 1
    <frame>let a = 2;</frame>         <- state 2: the tokens morph 1 -> 2
  \</code>
  <code lang=py>print("independent")\</code>   <- another block, no relation

  <h1>Title</h1>                     <- outside the sections: stays on screen
  <section> ... \</section>          <- a slide can swap whole sections under it
</code>

<p>
  Blocks with the <b>same <c>id</c></b> are one animation, in the same place, even across sections and slides:
  that is how code keeps morphing while the text around it changes.
</p>

<notes>
  The most important idea of the tool. Take your time here.
</notes>
